    "q-158052": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158052",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x+24\\pi )=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255329/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x+24\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 12) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x+24\\pi )=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
