    "q-136545": {
      "dom_id": "step-q136545",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255329/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
